{"type":"message","id":"55672a2b","parentId":"6137049f","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:28:39.668Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"read_ts_AY89a1-LsjqaxrRpISy_drmdn9xxK6uFcekXipgHW7X4BiurGcUCcnF9u32VsfW8tMtRDoRxRcVASqnRYvS3BwQ172_TtzN0V42hFcF1qSYrX6oK7JJVz_GQnqqi5QLvFzyA5c7yhzU72FEss_Tlslam881exWJCusWNDvim0XufVIGqdBW-3BgR1SA4NPV6dqTy0qPgbLRnwxGxRVajh7TiW0C1GG10qkSRTEwFyV2Ohs8whxw-RfYjRBHC8Ps5dlmYpeSQmi-IEov5-mm6GqgWDnKar4GeV5q-gLn_gebppysOVhFb7fwz0nIzXV2oTPb2rSw_i5ZtCXpR4pQTmb2BaN5IwC2Zjtou4xSima8y3y2dyJQB1D_R_vvOUQteLYwpqUhkNc4-6sy1NRdNHEAcOZe1z7EDShe6zTz9eILZIjSuvt2r0E4RabhuZ_9fQYejcchJcWEKsLGKC7oVruRzKCF22qV6Dscycrw2EzuXUaSU4ytcow","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"uk-sanctions-htx-russia-2026-05-27\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"UK Sanctions HTX Over Russia Ties\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"UK sanctions HTX\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"UK Imposes Sweeping Sanctions on HTX and Russia Crypto Networks\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"TRON\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"tron\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinDesk\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://decrypt.co/369047/uk-issues-sanctions-against-justin-sun-htx-crypto-firms-russia-ties\",\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/business/2026/05/26/uk-sanctions-huobi-and-ruble-stablecoin-issuer-in-crackdown-on-russia-crypto-networks\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"The United Kingdom announced a sweeping package of sanctions on Tuesday, May 26, 2026, targeting 18 entities and individuals accused of helping Russia evade Western sanctions, including Huobi Global S.A., the operator of the HTX crypto exchange.\",\n    \"For the first time, the UK applied Regulation 17A of its Russia sanctions regime—a banking-style restriction—to crypto exchanges, legally requiring British virtual asset service providers (VASPs) to freeze funds connected to the designated entities and trace transactions.\",\n    \"The UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office specifically accused HTX of providing financial services to the Kremlin-backed A7 payments network, which allegedly moved over $90 billion in 2025/2026 to process Russian oil sale proceeds and support military procurement.\",\n    \"HTX, which reported over $3 trillion in trading volume last year, stated that Huobi Global S.A. is legally distinct from the online HTX exchange and that global operations remain unaffected and user funds are safe.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"The United Kingdom has announced a sweeping package of sanctions against entities and individuals accused of helping Russia evade Western restrictions and finance its war in Ukraine, including the operator of the major crypto exchange HTX (formerly Huobi). The UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) sanctioned 18 entities and individuals linked to what officials described as Russia’s 'illicit financial infrastructure used to move funds, procure goods, and sustain its war.'\\n\\nAmong the sanctioned entities is Huobi Global S.A., the operator of the HTX exchange. HTX is one of the world’s largest cryptocurrency exchanges, with roughly $3 trillion to $3.3 trillion in trading volume last year, according to data from blockchain analytics firm Elliptic. The UK government accused HTX of providing financial services to A7, a Russian crypto exchange with its own ruble-pegged stablecoin (A7A5), and the Kremlin-backed A7 payments network, which officials say moved more than $90 billion last year to process proceeds from Russian oil sales and support military procurement. HTX is also suspected of providing services to Garantex, a Russian crypto exchange previously sanctioned by Western authorities.\\n\\nBritain's foreign secretary, Yvette Cooper, stated on Tuesday: 'If the Kremlin thinks it can evade our sanctions by hiding behind crypto networks and shadow financial systems, it is gravely mistaken.'\\n\\nThe sanctions mark a historical first, with the UK applying Regulation 17A of its Russia sanctions regime—a banking-style sanction tool previously used against sanctioned traditional banks—to cryptocurrency exchanges. Under these rules, UK financial institutions and virtual asset service providers (VASPs) are legally prohibited from maintaining correspondent relationships with the designated entities or processing payments tied to them. Crucially, VASPs must freeze funds connected to the designated exchanges and trace transactions. According to Elliptic, this could require firms to trace transactions across multiple blockchain 'hops', extending compliance checks beyond direct counterparties to any wallets and exchanges appearing anywhere in a transaction chain.\\n\\nIn response, an HTX spokesperson told Decrypt: 'The HTX exchange is committed to full compliance with all applicable laws and to cooperation with law‑enforcement agencies worldwide. The U.K.’s designation arrived today without prior notice or any supporting evidence shared with us.' The spokesperson clarified that 'the listed entity Huobi Global S.A. is distinct from the online HTX exchange. While Huobi Global S.A. will work with relevant UK authorities to understand the basis for the action and to address any concerns promptly, the designation does not and should not have any impact on the online HTX exchange.' HTX assured users that global operations are unaffected and user funds are safe.\\n\\nThe sanctions package also targeted other firms, including Rapira Group LLC, Aifory LLC, Arvix LLC, and Bitpapa IC FZC LLC. It also sanctioned Open Joint Stock Company 'Virtual Asset Issuer', a Kyrgyzstan-linked company behind the USDKG gold-backed stablecoin, along with several individuals accused of sanctions-evasion activity, including Sergey Mendeleev, Igor Gorin, Irina Akopyan, and Israeli national Liran Cohen.\\n\\nThese measures represent one of the UK's most aggressive actions to date against Russia's use of alternative payment networks and digital assets. They took effect immediately on Tuesday, May 26, 2026.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779863319668}}
